Debian package of VASSAL
------------------------

To build this Debian package somewhat cleanly, I by-passed the
upstream build system (Maven) entirely, and hand-crafted the build
directly in debian/rules.

The package will integrated in to the desktop automatically.  The
shell executable has been updated to be more flexible than upstream.

Reminder: To make new Debian release, do

   DEBEMAIL=... \
      dch -release-heuristic log --no-auto-nmu -i "..."

Installing a downloaded .deb
----------------------------

Install it with apt, not with dpkg:

   sudo apt install ./vassal_3.8.0-1_all.deb

The leading "./" matters: without it, apt reads the argument as the name
of a package to fetch from the archive rather than as a file.

"dpkg -i vassal_3.8.0-1_all.deb" installs only the file it is handed.  It
reports any unmet dependencies, but it leaves the package on the system
in that state, so VASSAL starts and looks healthy until it first needs a
class from the library that is missing.

Since 3.8.0 the library this is most likely to be is libxz-java, used for
the XZ-compressed saved-game format.  It is needed as soon as any module
is opened, so a 3.8.0 installed without it fails on opening any module
with

   java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: org/tukaani/xz/FilterOptions

and nothing before that point says a package is missing: the jars outside
/usr/share/vassal/lib are reached through Vengine.jar's Class-Path, whose
entries are symlinks into /usr/share/java, and the JVM silently ignores a
Class-Path entry that does not resolve.

To repair an installation already in that state:

   sudo apt --fix-broken install

Nothing needs reinstalling or reconfiguring afterwards; the symlink the
package already placed starts resolving as soon as the library is there.

This catches people upgrading from 3.7 in particular, because libxz-java
is the only dependency 3.8.0 added: every other library the package needs
is already present from the older version, so dpkg -i had always been
good enough before.
